11.4. Environmental Audits. Lessor, through its authorized representatives and
contractors, upon at least forty-eight (48) hours’ prior notice in writing to Lessee, at
any time and from time to time at its own cost and expense has the right to conduct
(or cause to be conducted) an environmental, health, safety, or security audit of all or
any portion of the Leased Site and the operations conducted thereon (including spill
prevention, process safety programs and Release records) to ensure compliance with
the provisions of the Lease Agreement. Lessor will cause such audit to be performed
in such a manner so as to minimize interference to the extent reasonably practicable
with Lessee’s operations.  Lessee has the right to have a representative present during
the performance of any such audit. Additionally, to the extent such disclosure would
not jeopardize any legal privilege, Lessee will provide Lessor with a copy of any report
and data with respect to any environmental audit or study of the Leased Site conducted
by or on behalf of Lessee that might impact Lessor or the community in the immediate
vicinity of the Lessor Adjoining Property. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the factual
basis of any audit finding, the contents of which might reasonably be expected to
impact Lessor or the community in the immediate vicinity of the Lessor Adjoining
Property, will be promptly disclosed to Lessor.

11.5. Environmental Definitions:

11.5.1. “Applicable Law” means any statute, law, regulation, ordinance, rule,
judgment, rule of law, order, decree, Permit, approval, concession, grant,
franchise, license, agreement, or requirement of any Governmental
Authority having or asserting jurisdiction over the matter or matters in
question, whether now or hereafter in effect and in each case as amended
(including, without limitation, any thereof pertaining to the environment,
land use or zoning restrictions and the common law of such Governmental
Authority), as well as any rights, duties, or obligations granted to, or
imposed upon any Person under federal or state common law including, but
not limited to, the common law of trespass and nuisance.

11.5.2. “Contaminant” means asbestos, lead, and any chemical, compound,
material, substance or other matter that: (a) is a flammable, corrosive,
explosive, hazardous, toxic or regulated material, substance or waste, or
other injurious or potentially injurious material, whether injurious or
potentially injurious by itself or in combination with other materials; or (b)
